#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628 :topic/difficulty 0.8280730284227736 :topic/id #uuid "5a0d0fb8-c0a3-55dc-a497-9f0d5f0eda76" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064203 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064071 :question/id #uuid "eff323d1-4e70-5490-8e41-9fbf00c387f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-446" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int (3x+1)^3 \\,  \\textrm{d}x .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(3x+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 4(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(3x+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2b47545-fdf1-5e6a-b0bb-bbbecb8067cc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Positive Integer Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064204 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064072 :question/id #uuid "e469c042-9619-55d4-b745-d4ed26251d80" :question/math-academy-id "e-447" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2}{(1-x)^3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2(1-x)^{-3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =  -\\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2(1-x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{u}^{-3}\\cdot (-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int {u}^{-3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{-2}}{-2}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =(1-x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-2(1-x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064075 :question/id #uuid "e416cb5c-22df-55e4-a3cb-c6b5dfc650c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-448" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\sqrt{1-5x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-5x)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-5x)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :topic/math-academy-id 334 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101}] :topic/title "Integrating Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675 :topic/difficulty 0.9247314504586539 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428831 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 303->1109. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "5e05a47e-5905-5eee-b6ea-48c720a8d288" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064365 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064009 :question/id #uuid "75114520-8891-503c-81b7-be4c86f5d054" :question/math-academy-id "e-202" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\arctan (-7x).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\arctan x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-7x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(-7x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+49{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{1+49{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9911dc0d-905d-54e1-aadb-f4fbce6f89d2" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Tangent Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064366 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064010 :question/id #uuid "c0a82f3e-9e04-50ce-b3b2-54fafd4c1db8" :question/math-academy-id "e-203" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ if $y = \\arccos (5-8x).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arccosine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\arccos x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(5-8x)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5-8x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5-8x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(25-80x+64{x}^{2})}}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{80x-24-64{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4(20x-6-16{x}^{2})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2\\sqrt{20x-6-16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{20x-6-16{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93a44f44-0998-5857-9b12-de8998e370fd" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Cosine Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064367 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064011 :question/id #uuid "0f8652d9-5d83-5d2c-93ae-2f3a4fc4e3e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-204" :question/problem "If $y= x \\arccos x,$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and using the formula for the derivative of $\\arccos x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x) & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x)+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\right)+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5582e8b-3870-5a32-b15e-62b8f8306ce6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Trigonometric Functions Using the Product or Quotient Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064368 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064012 :question/id #uuid "cbeacfa8-783e-5761-a74a-50b78a19ce20" :question/math-academy-id "e-206" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = 5\\arcsin(3x^2).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arcsine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\arcsin x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3{x}^{2})^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{4}}}\\cdot (6x) \\\\  & =\\frac{30x}{\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "552973a7-4ab5-5918-83f4-15ee0e3ee052" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Sine Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 303 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049402}] :topic/title "Differentiating Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}]}
